# Bug hunting in this pipeline

The owner is not an engineer and describes goals as business outcomes. Translate
those into technical checks. Never ask the owner to supply a failure scenario, a
file to inspect, or a test to write.

## Never invent a field

A contact detail is only ever recorded when it was read from the source. Never derive
one from a pattern, as the tools that compose `first.last@company.com` from a name and
a domain do: an address that looks right and bounces costs more than a blank field,
and it cannot be told apart from a real one by looking at the list.

The same holds for every other column. If Google's address carries no ZIP, the ZIP
stays empty rather than inheriting the one that was searched for. When a detail cannot
be found, the honest move is to leave it blank and widen the search, for instance by
following a contact link the site itself publishes.

## Evidence, not opinion

- Prove a bug with a script that runs and prints the wrong result, before fixing it.
- After fixing, revert the fix and show a test goes red. A fix no test protects is
  not finished.
- If something cannot be reproduced, report "not reproduced" rather than
  "possible risk". Reading code produces opinions; running code produces facts.
- Close by listing the assumptions that were not verified.

## Scenarios to work through

Each line is an outcome that would make the pipeline worthless to its owner. Derive
the technical check from it. Work through all of them; do not ask which to use.

- The same business appears twice in the list
- A business in the list has closed down
- A business has neither a phone number nor an email, so it cannot be contacted
- A business that should have been found is missing from the list
- A second run returns fewer leads than the first
- An area reported as searched was only partly searched
- The bill is larger than the number of leads can justify
- The CSV will not open or import cleanly
- A lead that was already contacted gets contacted again

## Where stale verdicts hide

`status`, `website_live` and the rows in `scrape_runs` are stored conclusions, not
live values. Any change touching the data a conclusion rests on must also invalidate
that conclusion, or a later stage will trust a verdict whose basis is gone.

Most tests here build an empty database and run one stage once, so they cannot see
this class of bug. Exercise a second run, a run days later, an interrupted run, and
two stages at once.
